Z||000006||revin||12-14-2001||07:33 AM||||My thought is if he doesn't know what the bleeder looks like he might have accidently messed with something other than the calipers. like the brake line and introduced air into the system. Or a hole in the line....any "wet" spots in the brake systems?||||reg||1
Z||000007||fierohoho||12-14-2001||03:59 PM||||This is a picture of the front passenger side caliper, the bleeder is on the left of the brake hose.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Brake_Bleeder.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/lightbar.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||CountFiero||12-14-2001||05:05 PM||||What hasn't been mentioned YET is that Once you have found the bleeder valves, they are USUALLY stuck on so DAMNED tight that you can barely open them to bleed the lines.  Use EXTREME care and ALOT of WD-40 to soak the valves BEFORE trying to open them.  Otherwise you will "round off" the hex on the valve and then you're in Deep S--T.<BR>BLEEDING is a job that's best done with the help of a friend anyway, unless you use a tube and a jar filled with brake fluid. There are several posts in the archive regarding this process.  Judging from the photo posted just above this reply Mine aren't the only one's that look like they are buried in rust and road grime. It seems that road salt is the WORST offender when it comes down to getting the valves "fused" to the caliper housing. If you do happen to round off the hex part.  Someone did suggest using a vice grip to get a good hold on the valve in order to turn it open. It sounds BASIC but Remember that turning LEFT (counterclockwise) is LOOSE / OPEN. Once you get the old valve off replace it with a new one and bleed as usual.  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||solo2m4||12-14-2001||08:34 PM||||If your brakes look like that than bleeding is going to be a bear I have a friend in a shop he told my he could bleed my brakes. He did It took 2 hours a tourch and alot of @#!%$^@ing since than I just replace the caliper's and brake fluid at the same time it dose cost much more and the brakes will always be in tip top shape, and there will be no vicegrips or @#%$#ing. You should never scimp on your brakes please I could want to race you some day ||||reg||12
Z||000010||theogre||12-15-2001||12:35 AM||||I'm sorry,,,, if you don't know what a bleeder screw is then get someone else to do your brake work. Please. Before you kill someone.<P>I doubt you'll get the screws open. Unless they are maintained, they will corrode solid. Because of it being steel in aluminum they often develope "electrolytic" corosion just like marine applications. this effectivly welds the bleeders in place.<P>At the very least this system is in desperate need a fluid flush and likely the rear calipers adjusted. Odds are good one or both rear calipers have internal problems that will require replacement of the pistons.<P>In either of the above cases the calipers will need to be replaced. By the time you try to fix either, a retail rebuilt part will be way cheaper. I'm told Autozone has them for about $30 each for rears.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000011||fierohoho||12-15-2001||10:44 AM||||My '84 had been sitting for 11 years when I got it and I was able to break loose the bleeders as follows.<P>#1-use a small drill bit "by hand" to clean  out the center of the bleeder.<P>#2-use a small wire brush to clean the visible threads on the bleeder.<P>#3-spray liquid wrench (LW) on the threads and push the small tube into the bleeder all the way and fill it with LW<P>#4-tap on the bleeder lightly with a wrench or something, this is not to loosen the bleeder but to make vibrations which will help the LW penetrate, do this for a minute or so.<P>#5-repeat steps 3-4 once a day for a total of 3 days.<P>#6-next I used a 6-point socket and a 3/8" breaker bar to first try to tighten the bleeder a hair and then to loosen it, when it does break loose don't force it out all the way if it still binds but turn it in and out to help loosen it up.<P>This worked on all 4 bleeders for me but maybe I just got lucky, and that bleeder in the pic, it turns without a problem.<P>I also make it a habit to crack the bleeders when the car comes out of winter storage<BR>every year as my wife holds the pedal down just to keep them loose.<BR>||||reg||1
